{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787060948,"nonce":"6okicurFkvKNqi5Skp95OE5TFJ4=","message":"**Amended: storm is removed ENTIRELY, not kept-but-undamped.** Done-when 3 and 4 above are reversed by this comment — `storm_mode`, `storm_since` and the channel notice do NOT remain.\n\nReason, from the owner: storm collapse is a defence against a problem whose real object does not exist yet. A storm is many DIFFERENT alerts arriving together; the thing that should own it is an incident, and `correlation` is DEFERRED-POST-V1. Building the defence before the object means the defence has nowhere to put what it detects, so it puts it in the notification layer — which is how it became damping in the first place. Removing it now and reintroducing it beside incidents is cheaper than carrying a half-feature that pretends the object exists.\n\nAlso correcting the question that prompted this: storm is a fact about a GROUP (`alert_groups.storm_mode`, `Group.StormMode()`), never about a case. A case is one alert firing once and cannot storm.\n\n**What removal actually reaches** — larger than this ticket first said, and it crosses the settings contract:\n\n- `db/migrations/00008_grouping.sql:38-39,61,87` — `storm_mode`, `storm_since`, `groups_storm_ck`\n- `db/migrations/00027_channel_storm_notice.sql` — `channels.storm_notice_at`\n- org settings: the storm policy is operator-tunable, so `internal/identity/{domain,api,repository,service}` all carry it — `domain/settings.go`, `api/{dto,mapper,router,settings}.go`, `repository/orgs.go`, `service/reads.go`. That is an API contract change, not an internal one.\n- `internal/ingestion/service/process.go` (evaluation), `internal/grouping/domain/group.go` (`EvaluateStorm`, `DefaultStormPolicy`), `internal/notification/service/notify.go:500`, `internal/notification/domain/{mode.go:255,broadcast.go:148}`, `internal/notification/repository/snapshot.go`, `internal/drill/domain/observe.go`, `internal/app/{container,routes,adapters}.go`\n- `docs/design/SPEC.md` §B.6\n\n**Revised Done-when, replacing 1-6:**\n1. `suppressed_reason` admits `no_policy` and `throttled` only.\n2. No notification is withheld, downgraded or broadcast because of storm. A test asserts a burst of arrivals produces one notification per triggering change.\n3. `storm_mode`, `storm_since`, `groups_storm_ck` and `channels.storm_notice_at` are dropped, with the org settings key removed from the contract and its OpenAPI surface regenerated.\n4. `ModeBroadcastReply` and `WarrantsChannelNotice`: check whether `mode.go:255` sets broadcast for any reason OTHER than storm before deleting either. If storm is its only caller, both go; if not, only the storm branch goes. Report which.\n5. **`ReasonStorm` is RETIRED, not deleted.** `notifications.reason` holds up to 13 months of `storm` rows. Follow the pattern `00051` used for `group.member_*` and `00054` used for `case.reopened`: the constant stays, stays decodable, stays in the persisted-spellings expansion, and the writer refuses it. Deleting it from the enum would make oto reject its own history.\n6. SPEC §B.6 is rewritten to say storm detection belongs with incidents and does not exist until they do — not merely that it is disabled.\n7. Depends on the case-retention ticket for flapping. Storm removal itself has no dependency.\n\n**Scope** is now medium→large, on the settings contract rather than on volume.","files":null}]}